1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,061
Welcome to lectures eleven and twelve:

2
00:00:03,061 --> 00:00:04,834
multivariate analysis.

3
00:00:04,834 --> 00:00:14,904
We cover multicollinearity and V I F, then discovering groups with clustering and K-Means, and how to interpret the clusters.

4
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:19,007
So far we compared features two at a time.

5
00:00:19,007 --> 00:00:29,701
Real datasets rarely explain themselves through a single pair; patterns often appear only when several features are considered together.

6
00:00:29,701 --> 00:00:31,431
Two things can happen.

7
00:00:31,431 --> 00:00:39,294
Redundant features carry almost the same information, wasting model capacity and distorting results:

8
00:00:39,294 --> 00:00:41,339
that is multicollinearity.

9
00:00:41,339 --> 00:00:49,753
And records can form hidden groupings across several features at once, invisible in any single scatterplot:

10
00:00:49,753 --> 00:00:52,112
that is what clustering finds.

11
00:00:52,912 --> 00:01:04,442
Multicollinearity occurs when one feature can be predicted with high accuracy from a combination of the others, so it adds little independent information.

12
00:01:04,442 --> 00:01:14,624
It distorts distance-based methods like clustering, inflates the importance of a repeated signal, and makes results harder to interpret.

13
00:01:14,624 --> 00:01:16,945
A pairwise check is not enough:

14
00:01:16,945 --> 00:01:24,657
two features can look uncorrelated individually, yet still be predictable from several others together.

15
00:01:24,657 --> 00:01:30,048
We need a measure that compares each feature to all the others combined.

16
00:01:30,048 --> 00:01:31,096
That is V I F.

17
00:01:31,896 --> 00:01:35,349
V I F equals one over one minus R squared.

18
00:01:35,349 --> 00:01:42,336
For each feature, we fit a mini-regression predicting it from all the other features.

19
00:01:42,336 --> 00:01:50,638
If the others predict it well, R squared is high and V I F is high, meaning the feature is redundant.

20
00:01:50,638 --> 00:01:55,242
A V I F of one means no correlation with other features.

21
00:01:55,242 --> 00:01:59,270
One to five is moderate and generally acceptable.

22
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:02,887
Five to ten is high and worth investigating.

23
00:02:02,887 --> 00:02:09,792
Above ten is severe multicollinearity, and you should consider dropping the feature.

24
00:02:10,592 --> 00:02:20,145
Once we have confirmed the features are independent enough to trust, a natural next question is about the rows, not the columns:

25
00:02:20,145 --> 00:02:27,907
do orders naturally cluster into distinct kinds, without us telling the data what those kinds should be?

26
00:02:27,907 --> 00:02:31,639
In supervised learning we already know the labels.

27
00:02:31,639 --> 00:02:41,192
In unsupervised learning, or clustering, no labels exist, and the algorithm groups records purely by similarity across features.

28
00:02:41,992 --> 00:02:44,812
In clustering, no labels are given.

29
00:02:44,812 --> 00:02:52,626
The algorithm discovers structure using only how similar records are across the numeric features.

30
00:02:52,626 --> 00:02:55,285
We use it here purely to explore:

31
00:02:55,285 --> 00:02:59,796
does structure exist, and what distinguishes each group?

32
00:02:59,796 --> 00:03:08,416
We are not building a predictive model or classifying new orders, just describing the data we already have.

33
00:03:09,216 --> 00:03:11,466
K-Means follows five steps.

34
00:03:11,466 --> 00:03:15,049
Choose k, the number of groups to look for.

35
00:03:15,049 --> 00:03:19,965
Place k centres, initially at random, in the feature space.

36
00:03:19,965 --> 00:03:23,464
Assign every record to the nearest centre.

37
00:03:23,464 --> 00:03:28,131
Update each centre to the average position of its group.

38
00:03:28,131 --> 00:03:33,713
Repeat the assign and update steps until assignments stop changing.

39
00:03:33,713 --> 00:03:44,712
In this course we use it only to describe existing data; the centres summarise patterns, they do not predict labels for new records.

40
00:03:45,512 --> 00:03:49,120
K-Means measures how far apart two records are.

41
00:03:49,120 --> 00:04:01,019
If features are on very different scales, the largest-scale feature silently dominates that distance, and the algorithm clusters on that one feature alone.

42
00:04:01,019 --> 00:04:08,542
Sales ranges up to twenty two thousand dollars, while discount runs only from zero to point eight.

43
00:04:08,542 --> 00:04:18,368
The fix is Standard Scaler, which rescales every feature to mean zero and standard deviation one, so all features count equally.

44
00:04:19,168 --> 00:04:21,990
K-Means needs k decided in advance.

45
00:04:21,990 --> 00:04:25,377
The silhouette score helps judge a choice:

46
00:04:25,377 --> 00:04:33,037
for each point it compares how close it is to its own cluster versus the nearest other cluster.

47
00:04:33,037 --> 00:04:39,408
Minus one is poor separation, zero is ambiguous, plus one is strong separation.

48
00:04:39,408 --> 00:04:46,181
Here k equals two scores highest, but only splits orders into smaller versus larger.

49
00:04:46,181 --> 00:04:53,277
k equals three is a close second and yields three clearly interpretable business groups.

50
00:04:53,277 --> 00:04:57,712
The statistically best k is not always the most useful.

51
00:04:58,512 --> 00:05:08,859
To see the clusters, colour a scatterplot by cluster using the hue argument for a quick check that clusters correspond to a visible pattern.

52
00:05:08,859 --> 00:05:19,872
A pairplot with hue shows every pair of features at once, each coloured by cluster, the fastest way to see separation across all dimensions together.

53
00:05:20,672 --> 00:05:23,983
A cluster number means nothing on its own.

54
00:05:23,983 --> 00:05:29,421
Interpretation reuses the grouped statistics from bivariate analysis:

55
00:05:29,421 --> 00:05:33,520
group by cluster instead of by an existing category.

56
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:38,250
Cluster zero, sixty nine hundred orders, is everyday orders.

57
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:45,896
Cluster two is high-value, high-profit orders averaging six hundred thirty four dollars in sales.

58
00:05:45,896 --> 00:05:48,418
And cluster one is the standout:

59
00:05:48,418 --> 00:05:56,694
heavy discounts averaging sixty six percent, with an average loss of one hundred seven dollars per order.

60
00:05:56,694 --> 00:06:00,084
That becomes a hypothesis to test formally:

61
00:06:00,084 --> 00:06:03,316
is this discount policy destroying value?

62
00:06:03,316 --> 00:06:08,912
Cross-tabulate clusters against an existing category as a sanity check.

63
00:06:09,712 --> 00:06:10,873
Four cautions.

64
00:06:10,873 --> 00:06:17,508
K-Means is not a prediction model in this course; it describes existing records.

65
00:06:17,508 --> 00:06:20,162
And k is chosen, not discovered:

66
00:06:20,162 --> 00:06:24,391
the silhouette score only helps compare candidates.

67
00:06:24,391 --> 00:06:31,357
It is sensitive to scale and outliers, so always scale first and check for outliers.

68
00:06:31,357 --> 00:06:34,094
And clusters need a sanity check:

69
00:06:34,094 --> 00:06:40,480
a statistically tight cluster is only useful if it also makes business sense.

70
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:42,421
Key takeaways.

71
00:06:42,421 --> 00:06:46,985
Multivariate analysis studies several features together.

72
00:06:46,985 --> 00:06:57,499
Multicollinearity means a feature is largely redundant given the others, checked with correlation and more rigorously with V I F:

73
00:06:57,499 --> 00:07:03,530
near one is ideal, above five is worth investigating, above ten is severe.

74
00:07:03,530 --> 00:07:09,724
K-Means discovers groups without labels, for exploration and not prediction.

75
00:07:09,724 --> 00:07:11,273
Always scale first.

76
00:07:11,273 --> 00:07:18,527
The silhouette score compares values of k, but the highest is not always the most useful.

77
00:07:18,527 --> 00:07:28,144
And a cluster is meaningful only after you interpret it with grouped statistics and check it against known categories.

78
00:07:28,144 --> 00:07:29,448
Next is the lab.

